\section{Related work and prior art differentiation}
We position this work relative to three neighboring literatures and to core LLM prompting methods.

\paragraph{Prompted reasoning and search in LLMs.}
CoT \cite{wei2022cot} and self-consistency \cite{wang2023selfconsistency} improve LLM reasoning via verbalized steps and multiple samples; tree-of-thought search \cite{yao2023tot} adds branching, while debate-style prompting \cite{irving2018debate} explores multi-agent critique. Our focus is complementary: we provide a \emph{compute allocation} theory (BPF/EVC) and algorithms that schedule deliberation adaptively, with risk-sensitive controls and index-guided policies.

\paragraph{Metareasoning and limited rationality.}
We build upon metareasoning \cite{russellwefald1991,horvitz1989,liedergriffiths2020}, bandit indices \cite{gittins1979}, submodularity \cite{nemhauser1978}, and adaptive submodularity \cite{golovin2011adaptive}. Our contributions connect these principles to inference-time LLM control and provide new sufficient conditions for indexability and anytime guarantees.

\paragraph{Out-of-scope areas and differentiation.}
Our setting and objectives are distinct from several adjacent domains:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=0.2em]
\item Data-center right-sizing optimizes power/latency and capacity pools \cite{lin2011rightsizing}; we allocate \emph{cognitive actions} within a single-instance deliberation.
\item Intertemporal risk economics studies preferences over consumption streams \cite{kreps1978temporal}; we study algorithmic risk in search selection.
\item Health-disparities research (e.g., BRCA1/2 uptake \cite{armstrong2005racial}) concerns access and policy; our work is a computational control framework for LLM reasoning.
\end{itemize}

\section{Methodology}
Our methodology integrates normative optimization with algorithmic blueprints.

\paragraph{Formal target.}
We adopt the BPF $P(B)$ as the performance target under a compute budget $B$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:bpf}, and use EVC (Eq.~\eqref{eq:evc}) as a local decision quantity.

\paragraph{Scheduling principles.}
We unify:
(i) cost-benefit greedy under (adaptive) submodularity for anytime near-optimality;
(ii) index policies for multi-thread scheduling (IGD) with Whittle-style relaxations;
(iii) risk-sensitive selection (RS-MCTT) using entropic risk measures in UCT-like exploration \cite{kocsis2006uct,ahmadi2012entropic};
(iv) dual decomposition for compute markets (DRSM);
(v) admissible bounding for verifiable tasks (BB-LLM).

\paragraph{Evaluation plan.}
We evaluate methods along the BPF, marginal value-of-compute (MVC), reliability at fixed budget, and diversity/verification/memory efficiency. A synthetic illustration is provided (Section~\ref{sec:synthetic}).

